# Line-ending policy.
#
# Without this file, a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true stores CRLF in
# the repository. Linux CI then sees every text file as modified, and
# `ruff format --check` fails on files nobody touched.
#
# Force LF in the repository and let git hand the working tree whatever the
# local platform wants. This is the one setting that makes "passes on my
# machine" mean something on the runner.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must keep LF even when checked out on Windows, or bash refuses
# to run them.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts keep CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary assets: never touch these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.pdf binary

# The screenshots under docs/screens/ are PNGs and are matched above.
# favicon.svg is text and stays normalised like the rest.

# Generated data. These are written by Python and diffed by humans, so keep
# them on LF too and let git decide the working-tree form.
*.json text
*.yaml text
*.yml text
*.md text
*.css text
*.js text
*.mjs text
*.html text
*.svg text
*.py text
*.toml text

# Make the language stats on the repository page honest: these are vendored
# fixture snapshots, not source we wrote.
tests/fixtures/*.md linguist-vendored
